The cost is real but it is linear and well behaved: a 7,000-token prompt takes two seconds to read before a single word comes back. For an agent, which chapter 21 measured being read to **49× more than it speaks**, those two seconds are most of the wall clock. ## Then the engine said no The next step up was an 8,800-token prompt, comfortably inside the 16,384 the engine had been told to allocate and comfortably inside what chapter 23's 8-bit cache pays for. Measured · what came back instead 
 {"error":{"code":400,"message":"request (8800 tokens) exceeds the available context size (8192 tokens)"}} 
 Asked for 16,384. Allowed 8,192. And the memory was not the reason — chapter 23 measured an 8-bit cache at 8,192 tokens costing 570 MB on a card with gigabytes free.

It would have inflated every tokens-per-second number in Part I by 43%, consistently, across all five engines — so every comparison would still have looked right, and the error would have survived to the end of the project. This is the class of mistake that does not announce itself. ```python
body = json.dumps({
 "model": model, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": prompt}],
 "max_tokens": max_tokens, "temperature": temperature, "stream": True,
 # Ask the engine for REAL token counts. "stream_options": {"include_usage": True},
}).encode()
``` And when the engine refuses to say, the harness records **which source it fell back to**, because a number is only as good as its provenance: ```python
if usage and usage.get("completion_tokens"):
 out_tok, source = usage["completion_tokens"], "engine"
elif chunks:
 out_tok, source = chunks, "chunks" # one chunk per token, usually
else:
 out_tok, source = len(text) / 4, "chars/4" # last resort, and it is poor
```

